package pp

// The out pages are the longer reads a foray leaves a bird with, kept in
// their book at the gate (LORE.md, how a day out reads). Every event has a
// page, given the first time a bird meets it: what the place is, who works
// it, what it costs and who it's owed to. Every class arc has an epilogue,
// given once its third stage is told. They're the world told from inside
// it, and they're long on purpose: reading them is half of going out.
//
// Keyed by place, then by event key, or by class for an epilogue.

// pages are each event's page, by place and event.
var pages = map[string]map[string]string{
	"mire": {
		"cutting": "the old cutting below the hedge was the city's own until forty years ago, dug by any household that could carry a spade down the cart track and sold to nobody, burned in its own grate. then the counting house lent the cutters' guild money against a bad winter, the guild couldn't pay, and the cutting went to the house, which sold it on to three kiln families and a partner of its own, who called themselves the consortium. the cutters still cut it. they're paid by the stack, and a stack is measured on the tallyman's board, which is measured by the consortium, which owns the board. the cutters keep their own count on notched sticks hung in the turf hut at the top of the cart track, and every season the two counts are a little further apart. the oldest cutters remember when peat was given away to anybody too poor to buy it. the youngest have only known the board. the coot who cuts the step nearest the hedge and her brother below her are the last pair on the cutting whose family cut it before the house bought it, and the consortium has offered twice to buy their spades. they keep the spades.",
		"reeds": "the reed beds are the near reaches' real trade, worth more to the city than the peat. every thatched roof in the sump is mire reed, every backcloth in the bellward playhouses is painted on reed paper, and every bed in a cliff house is a reed mattress changed at midsummer. the cutters work in pairs, a cutter and a binder, and a pair is paid by the bundle at the cart. they sing to keep the scythe and the twine in time, and the songs are older than anybody can date: forty verses about the families on the stair, who climbed and who fell, with a new verse whenever a family does something the mire hears about. nobody knows how the mire hears. the choir has taken its best tunes from the beds for a hundred years and pays nothing for them, and the cutters say that's fine, because the choir has never got the words right. there are verses the cutters won't sing with anyone from the pot in earshot. there's one about the drowned-bell family that every cutter knows and none of them will say who wrote.",
		"eels": "the eel men walk the weirs on stilts taller than a cutter, and have done since before the hedge: the stilts are older than the city and so is the trade. a weir is a line of stakes and wicker across a run, with a trap at every gap, and an eel man's weirs are his family's, handed down and never sold. the sump buys eels by the basket at the fish market, stewed and jellied and smoked, and the gilt buys them too and calls them something else on the menu. the eel men don't use pp among themselves. they trade by the basket and keep the account on sticks, and they'll trade with anyone for anything: a knife, a coat, an hour carrying traps. the consortium has tried to buy the weirs to drain the runs for cutting, and the eel men have said no in the eel men's way, which is that the consortium's surveyors keep walking into the deep water by mistake. they say the eels are getting longer. they say it the way they'd say the price of a basket was up.",
		"mat": "a mat is moss and root grown so thick over open water that it floats, and in the near reaches some of them are as big as the pot square. they rise and fall with the mire, and a mat that took a laden cart last spring will take nothing this one. the cutters say it depends on the year and the eel men say it depends on the mat, and the hedge company, which lost a patrol through one in the rising, says it depends on nothing and set marker poles round every mat it could find. the poles are numbered, but not by the company: somebody renumbers them every winter, out of order, and the company has stopped repainting them. under the moss the peat is warm. it's warm in the coldest week of the year, warm enough to put a hand on, and the hearthkeepers say it's the bog breathing, and the kiln keepers say it's the same heat they burn, given away for free.",
		"house": "the hearthkeepers' house stands on the one hummock at the mire's western edge high enough to stay dry in the rising, which is why it was built there, and why the herons say it was built at all. it's a long low house of grey stone with a slate roof weighted down with stones against the mire's wind, a chapel at one end, a dormitory at the other and peat stacked against every wall to the eaves. its novices stand in the water in front of it every day for a year, to the knee in summer and the shin in winter, because the house teaches that standing is the first discipline and everything else follows from it. the house buys the city's hearth peat from the cutters by the cartload, and it also takes peat a bird has cut with their own hands, which the herons lay on the hearth themselves and the notch stick counts as the day's work poured in. the abbot is old and doesn't come to the city. he writes letters instead, and every one of them is signed with a fire that's never been let out.",
		"kilns": "the kilns stand on the old line of the eastern hedge, five of them in a row, brick bottles taller than a house with their mouths always red. they burn peat day and night and blow pale glass from its ash: the bellward's windows, the gilt's lamps and the glass perches the gilt stands on to be seen. the eastern hedge was sold to the consortium by the hedge company nine years ago, the stretch thinned for the cutting beyond it and the patrol withdrawn, and on the third night something came through the gap that the company won't name. a squad of shrikes died holding it. the consortium paid the company for the thorn and docked the squad's pay for the lost pikes. the guard who sits on a barrel by the gap now is paid to watch it and not to stop anything that goes through, and sledges go through it most months, at night, heading south. the glass that comes out of the kilns is very fine.",
		"ledger": "the bog keeps things. that's the first thing a cutter learns and the thing the city least believes until it sees it. a body put down in the mire comes back up a hundred years later with its face still on, a knife comes up sharper than it went down and paper comes up legible, ink and all, as long as it stays wet. the cutters have dug up shoes, kegs of butter still sweet, a whole cart with the horse's harness on it and no horse, and once a ledger of the old counting house branch from the low town, though the low town is under the sea, not the mire, and nobody can say how it came to be in a peat cutting. the cutters bury what they dig up again if they can, because what the bog gives back it expects back, they say. a ledger is the worst thing to find. anything a cutter digs up that's written in is taken by the tally board, and anything the tally board takes, it reads.",
		"lights": "the mire's lamps come out at dusk and go in before midnight, small lights low over the open water, never more than a few at once. they move against the wind and always away from the city. the hedge company put a patrol on them in the rising and lost it. the hearthkeepers say they're the bog breathing, the eel men say they're the eels, the cutters say nothing and don't look, and a raven from the counting house once wrote a pamphlet proving they were a trick of the light and was found the next spring standing in the mire to the waist, a long way out, with no memory of the winter. the choir has a song about them that the playhouses won't stage. the cutters have one rule: a bird who follows the lights and comes back gets a seat at the fire and a cup of something hot and nobody asks where they went. most do come back. the cutters keep a count of the ones who don't, on a stick in the turf hut, and the stick is very short.",
		"causeway": "the causeway is older than the hedge. it crosses the open mire on piles to the last wood, and it was built by somebody before the flock came to the spire, and every span of it since has been rebuilt by whoever needed it that year: the hedge company, the wardens, the north sea company when it wanted the wood's amber, the counting house when it wanted the wood's oak and was refused. so no two spans match. some are oak on oak piles and some are iron, one is cut stone, one is the bottom of a cart and one is a whaleboat laid upside down with its name scraped off. the hedge company keeps a gang on it at the city's expense to rebuild any span that goes, and they rebuild with whatever has floated up that month. under the causeway the drowned villages lie in rows, roofs down, from before the rising. the gang says you can hear them on still nights, and that what you hear is very ordinary: doors, a dog, somebody calling a child in.",
		"chapel": "seven villages stood on the mire's dry ground before the rising, each with its reeve and its chapel and its rents paid to the old counting house branch in the low town. the rising took them in a season, slowly: the water came up a hand a week and the villages moved their beds upstairs, then onto the roofs, then into boats, and then they walked to the hedge and the hedge company let them in through the north gate one family at a time. the wet guild keeps a book of all seven, every house and every strongbox, because a drowned village is salvage and salvage is the guild's. in a dry month the chapels' ridges come up out of the water like the backs of whales. the bell turrets still have their bells, and the bells are cracked, because the cold water cracks bronze. every cutter knows a dry summer is a bad summer, because the ridges come up and somebody always pulls a rope, and the mire answers.",
		"twice": "the bird the cutters dug up twice was a cutter himself, by his spade and his boots, and nobody knows his name. he was found first in the cutting in the eldest cutter's mother's time, on the step below the one her mother cut, lying on his side with his spade beside him as if he'd lain down to sleep. they put him back in the same place, the way the cutters always do, and cut round him for forty years. then the step he was on was cut down to by her daughter's gang, and there he was again, a little further down than they'd left him, and a little further along. they say he's walking. they say it the way they'd say he was heavy. his tally stick has nine notches, the consortium's brand and a date forty years before the consortium existed, and the coin in his pocket is from no mint the counting house will admit to. the cutters put him back on the step he came up from. they think next time it will be their grandchildren's gang.",
		"rising": "the great rising was before anybody now living, when the mire came up to the hedge in a single season and stood against the thorn for a month, and the hedge company held the thorn with pikes and the herons stood in the water at the four corners of the hedge until it stopped. the kiln keepers blew the first pale glass from the ash of what burned that month. small risings come every few years, a finger's width at a time, out of the reed with no wind behind them, and the cutters climb their stacks and wait. the hearthkeepers send novices out to stand in the water against them, as the old herons did, and nobody knows whether the standing does anything. the hearthkeepers don't claim it does. they say they stand because a rising is easier to bear when somebody is standing in it, and that the water has always stopped, and that it would have stopped anyway, and that this is not the same thing as saying the standing does nothing.",
	},
	"wood": {
		"count": "the count is the wardens' only law. every tree in the last wood has a number, painted white at a bird's eye height on its bark, and every season the wardens meet at the counting trees, a ring of oaks at the edge with a stump at its middle, and read the numbers aloud from a bark scroll, and a warden posted somewhere in the wood calls each one back. a number nobody calls back is a hole in the count, and a hole is the worst thing that can happen in the last wood. the count has gone up by one or two every season since the first winter. no warden has ever planted a tree, and they would know: a warden notices a seed. the city has never understood why the wardens care so much about a number, and the wardens have never explained, beyond saying that a wood you've counted is a wood you can tell is being taken from, and that the city learned what that was worth in the first winter.",
		"resin": "the oldest trees in the last wood are beeches and oaks split by winters nobody remembers, and they weep resin where they split, and the resin hardens on the bark into long gold beads and drops into the leaf litter as amber. the wardens' rule is painted on every weeping trunk as a white line at a bird's shoulder: above the line is the tree's and below it is anybody's who bends down. the wardens argue about what counts as fallen. a bead hanging by a thread of resin is fallen to some wardens and cut to others, and the arguments go on for seasons, and are settled, when they are, by the tree dropping it. amber is the wood's one honest trade. nothing may be cut and nothing may be dug, but what a tree has let go of, a bird may carry out, and the wardens let the sump's pickers sift the litter for it under a warden's eye because the pickers have nowhere else to earn in winter.",
		"shelter": "a warden lives in a shelter of bark and hide between the roots of a tree too big to fall, and never in a house. the wood is the only thing the wardens own, and they own it in common, and a warden's shelter is wherever her post is, and her post moves with the count. she keeps a fire in front of it on dropped wood, and the fire is never let out: the wardens' fires have been fed by somebody every night since the first winter, passed from shelter to shelter when a post moves. the city says wardens don't feed visitors. the truth is that visitors don't sit down. a bird who sits at a warden's fire and waits gets a cup of whatever is in the pot, which is usually mushrooms, and an answer to one question, which is usually the truth. the wardens copy the out pages from the gate onto bark every evening, so the wood keeps its own count of who's in it, and a warden can tell any bird where they are within a few trees.",
		"stump": "the oaks taken from the last wood go out at night, on sledges, across the mire, through the gap in the eastern hedge the consortium bought, up the kiln road and the stair to the gilt, where the fireplaces burn oak all winter. a warden on a cold trail followed the drag marks to the gilt three autumns ago and wrote a warrant on bark that no court in the city will hear. the wardens hang a strip of black cloth on every stump for the hole in the count, and there are eleven cloths in the wood now. the wardens won't cut a tree to replace one and won't plant one either. they say the count will close itself or it won't. the gilt says the oak comes from its own estates in the grey north, and the north sea company carries the timber, and the company's books show a cargo of oak from the north every autumn. the company's ships come back from the north with empty holds.",
		"pickers": "the pickers are sump children, mostly, sent up the wardens' road in gangs in the autumn with flour sieves and sacks, to sift the leaf litter under the old trees for the amber that's dropped. a gang can clear a pp a day each in a good autumn, more than a cliff house child earns any other way, and the wardens let them pick under a warden's eye because the wardens were the first to say the wood's dropped things belong to whoever bends down. the north sea company buys the pickers' amber at the wood's edge by the bag, at a price it chalks on a board, and the price is low. it's low because the company is the only buyer that comes this far. the slack tide's purser takes amber at the gannet's door in place of a candle, which the pickers know, and which is worth more, and which is in the sump, a day's walk with a sack, in a tavern the hearthkeepers have asked the children not to enter.",
		"buyers": "the north sea company is a whaling concern in the bellward that has been in the amber trade for as long as anyone has kept books on it. its clerks set up a table at the wood's edge every autumn with a scale and a strongbox, and buy by the bag. the company says it buys amber to varnish pale glass, and it does. it also ships amber north, casks of it, under the company's own seal, on its own ships, to a whalers' landing on the cliff road, and the casks come back empty in spring. the company's contract with the pickers says amber bought at the wood's edge is not to be sold in the city, and the company buys amber out of the slack tide's hold as well, at the hold's price, more than it pays the pickers. a raven of the counting house who looked into this was struck off for it. the company's clerks are polite and well paid and they have very good dice.",
		"brook": "the brooks that come down out of the deep wood run the colour of strong tea, stained by the litter they've come through, and in the slack water behind the stones the amber they've washed out of the banks lies on the bottom where anybody can see it and nobody can easily reach it. the water is cold all year. the wardens say the brooks come up out of the ground under the oldest trees and are the trees' own water, and a warden won't drink from a brook without asking which tree it came from first, though nobody has ever heard a warden say who she asks. in a dry autumn the wardens post themselves on the banks and close a brook to pickers for a month, and won't say why, and the next spring the trees along that brook drop twice the amber. the pickers have learned not to argue with a closed brook. the eels in the mire come up the brooks to spawn and are never seen coming back.",
		"painter": "the wardens paint the numbers in spring, all of them, every tree in the wood, a different warden every year carrying the paint pot from the edge to the deep wood and back, a season's walk. the paint is the wardens' own, lime and something else, and it doesn't wash off and doesn't fade. in autumn, sometimes, a bird on the path finds a number freshly painted, still wet, with nobody in sight in either direction, and the paint pot standing on the path between two trees. the wardens say that happens. they don't say who. a warden asked about it will say the count is kept, and that a wood that's been counted for that long knows how to count itself, and then she'll change the subject to mushrooms. the new numbers are always correct. the wardens check them, every one, and they've never found a wrong one, and they've stopped checking where anyone can see them do it.",
		"first": "in the first winter, when the first rookery had slid into the sea and the survivors had climbed to the spire's foot with nothing, the flock walked out across the mire on the old causeway and asked the wardens for timber, to build with and to burn. the wardens said no. they gave the flock the dropped wood and nothing else, and the flock went home and rendered its own fat for tallow and burned that, and a great many of them froze. the city has never forgiven the wardens and the wardens have never apologised. they say a wood that gives itself away in one winter is gone by the next, and that the city would have burned it all and frozen anyway, and that they were right. the flock asked for one tree first, a particular one, before it asked for the rest. the wardens know which. it has no number. the order of the stair holds a writ against the wood for it that has never been served.",
		"lost": "the wardens' own paths in the deep wood aren't marked, because the wardens don't need them marked and nobody else is meant to be on them. the numbers go up from the edge toward the deep wood, the wardens have told the city that much, but in the far reaches they go into the thousands and stop running in any order a bird from the city can follow: a hundred and twelve twice, four thousand next to nine, numbers with a bar under them that the wardens won't explain. a bird lost in the deep wood is meant to sit down and wait, and the wood will tell the wardens where they are. nobody knows how. the wardens copy the out pages onto bark every evening and know who's in the wood and where, and a warden finds a lost bird without looking for them, walks up to them in the trees and says their number from the out pages. no bird has been lost in the last wood for longer than a night.",
		"camp": "the first winter's camp is in a hollow in the deep wood, a ring of blackened stones round a lump of grey tallow half sunk in the ground, and the wardens say it's where the flock sat the night it was refused. they had walked out across the mire to ask for timber and were given dropped wood, and on the walk home they rendered fat at this fire to pour candles to light their way, and left the rest. the tallow is still soft. it's been soft for longer than the city has had a name, through every winter, and the wardens don't touch it and don't let a fire be lit in the ring. the chandlers on the row keep one mould for nothing else: the first winter's own, a plain round mould with a crack in it, that every chandler on the row has used once in a life, for one candle, which goes in the window and isn't lit. the chandlers say the first tallow perch was poured in that mould. nobody has ever checked.",
		"lump": "the oldest tree anybody has seen in the last wood is a beech split three ways and still alive, deep in the far reaches, and the wardens say it was old in the first winter. it drops amber the size of a bird's fist. some of what it drops has things in it: seeds and wasps, which is ordinary, and a pot point, which isn't, stamped on both faces like the ones in every purse in the city and caught in amber older than the pot. the counting house has asked the wardens three times for one of them and been refused three times. the purser at the slack tide has seen one and won't weigh it. the wardens say the beech dropped what it dropped and the pot is younger than the beech, and they leave it at that. the reckoners say a pot point in amber that old means the pot was minting before the city, or that the beech is younger than the wardens say, and the counting house has written both down and decided neither.",
	},
}

// epilogues are each class's arc at each place told out, given once its
// third stage is: what came of it, in that bird's own story.
var epilogues = map[string]map[string]string{
	"mire": {
		"fighter": "you've walked the eastern hedge from the barrel to the kilns more times now than you walked it in nine years of the company, and you know it the way you knew it then: by what's wrong with it. the thorn bent outward. the pike in the bank. the guard on the barrel who saluted. the consortium's partner who rode out the week the oak went through. the company called that night a regrettable loss. you've started calling it what it was, which is a sale with a squad thrown in. there are eight sticks on the thorn now and nine names on your list, because one of them the wood broke and the board won't let go, and the board will learn. the pits pay by the bout and the crowd. you've taken to wearing the clay to the kiln road on your days off and sitting on the barrel beside the guard, saying nothing, until the carters stop looking at you. he's started bringing two cups.",
		"monk": "you put the stick back exactly as it lay and nobody knows you read it but the porter, who locked the door after you and has never once spoken of it. the house's fire has been set a finger higher than the count, every week, by a stick with the counting house's seal on its cord, for as long as the stick goes back. somebody has been deciding when the city's hearth lights, and it isn't the herons, and it isn't the count. you don't know yet whether the abbot knows. you think he does. you think he cut the first stick, and that whoever holds the second has been reading the first. you've started cutting a stick of your own in the sump, notch by notch, for the fires you keep there, and reading its middle every morning before dawn the way you were taught. your fires light every night anyway. but now you know what they would have been, and so does the sump, because you tell them.",
		"ranger": "the name on the kiln office door is a name from the gilt, and you have counted its chimneys: oak from the first frost to the thaw, more than any household burns, and its kitchen boy selling the pale fine ash in the sump as soap. the sledges go out from the camp in the mire every month with twelve notches on the stick and a thirteenth in yours. the guard on the barrel doesn't stand up. you have it all now, the wood's count and the city's, from the stump to the chimney, and the only court in the city that will hear it is the wardens', and the wardens don't hang anybody. they told you to come home. you've written to them instead, on bark, with the name, and asked them to read it at the counting trees at the next count, aloud, the way they read a hole. a count with a hole in it isn't a count. a count with a name in it might be a start.",
		"wanderer": "the bosun's log is inside yours now, under the strap, with the eleven names. four of them are crossed out in the bosun's hand, the four who came south with the second boat and didn't make the hedge. the rest stayed with her, lamps lit. the company's books say the kittiwake was lost with all hands. the company's clerk has offered you a bellward perch twice to sign that it was. you've been to the pot's rim every week since and asked every albatross who comes in off the grey north the same question, and last spring one of them said yes, and now you have the bosun's fix to go with it: two days south of where she was held. it's a voyage. the counting house lends against a voyage north. you haven't signed. you don't intend to. the boat strap still smells of the mire, and you've stopped trying to wash it out.",
		"thief": "the key is on a cord round your neck under the red coat, the old counting house branch's own mark on its ring, and it opens a vault under the low town that has one more window than the wet guild's plan shows. you know which window. you've told the kingfisher and the gull and the raven with the branded hand, and nobody else. under the reeve's hearthstone in a drowned village in the mire there are hundreds of tally sticks, every debt the seven villages owed the branch the day the water came, and the bog will keep them a thousand years. under the low town there are more. the guild says it's a story. the guild kept the jeweller's window, and you've never said a word about that, and you won't, until the spring turn, when the tide runs lowest and the bell rings for whoever is down there. this time you intend to be.",
		"sorcerer": "the line at the bottom of the slip came clear in a week, as you said it would, and it's in your own cipher in another raven's hand: the consortium's partner from the counting house, as the rule. two stacks a day off the board. the kiln office's second book. the branded clerk's third. the missing share of the pot, taken only when the shop sits quiet and only from the heaviest purses, by the same rule, set by the same hand. you've got three seasons of it now in betting slips, and a brick of the second book's peat on your windowsill to show the second book exists. the counting house brands a bird once. you know what it does the second time, because the clerk in the shed told you, and it doesn't leave a mark. you've started keeping a fourth copy, in a fourth cipher, somewhere that isn't the sump.",
		"bard": "the new verse has eleven stacks and nine on the board in it, and the cutters sang it through the whole bed the evening you wrote it, and the next morning a cart driver sang it at the top of the cart track, and by the end of the week it was in the gin lanes. the consortium's tallyman heard it sung under his window and changed his board. he didn't change the count. he changed the board, so nobody could read it from the cutting. the cutters say that's how you know a verse has landed. the eldest told you the thirtieth verse came out of the mire with the peat, and you've started to believe her, because you've been listening to the bed sing and some nights it sings a line you haven't written yet, and the next week it's true. you'll sing the new one at the pot at dawn. the choir master has sent word, by the boy, that he'll be on the steps to hear it.",
		"knight": "the commander folded the receipt and the torn writ together and put them in his coat, and the bailiff's fee went to the moorhen's hut in the reed, and the moorhen's brother cut the next step with a new spade that you paid for out of your own purse and will never be repaid for. the order hasn't sent a third writ. the order has, however, moved your name on the chapter house roster one rung down, for a writ unserved, the one you crossed out at the hut door. your family went down the stair with what they could carry when the order served the writ on them. you've been down it one rung, carrying nothing but your own name in order blue, and it was lighter than you expected. you still keep the vigil's silence. the cutters have started bringing you their writs before the order does, to read them aloud, and to sign that you read them.",
	},
	"wood": {
		"ranger": "the count is whole. eleven stumps, eleven saplings, eleven numbers painted on new bark, and no warden planted any of them. the reader took the black cloths down one by one and gave them to you, and you keep them folded in your pack with the warrant nobody will hear. the gilt still burns oak. the sledges still cross the mire. the name on the kiln office door is still on the door. but the wood has its count back, and the wardens have your count in the counting trees' hollow on a strip of bark with your number on it, and the reader called your old numbers at the last count and waited for you to call them back, and you did. you still sleep on the city's rooftops. you've stopped thinking of it as being away from home. the wardens have stopped telling you to come back to the wood. they've started asking what the city's count says this week.",
		"wanderer": "to keep the lamps lit. the company has sent amber north every season since the kittiwake was lost, casks of it, because amber burns with no smoke and lasts in the cold, and the empty casks come back in spring. somebody in the ice is burning it. somebody has been keeping the kittiwake's lamps lit for years, and the company is paying for the oil, and billing you for the boat, and offering you a perch to sign that there's nobody up there to light anything. the clerk gave you a bead out of his own pocket. you put it in the log with the eleven names and the bosun's fix. it's a voyage north. the counting house lends against a voyage north, and the company insures every ship that goes. you've started asking the albatrosses at the rim a different question: not whether anyone has seen her, but who sails for the company's landing, and when.",
		"fighter": "the reader wrote it on the back of the scroll: the debt is not owed in the last wood. it isn't law anywhere else. the tally board still has the goshawk's name on its list and yours under it, and it has written to you twice more, and you've carried both letters into the wood and her mother has put both on her fire. there are eight sticks on the eastern thorn now and the broken one hangs beside them in two halves, which the hedge company men on the western stretch have started calling the warden's tally. they've started bringing you their dead squadmates' sticks, too, from the western hedge, to carry into the wood and have the reader write on the back of her scroll. you don't know if it changes anything. her mother says it changes the count, which is the only thing in the wood that matters, and that the city will catch up.",
		"sorcerer": "door to hold to company to pumps to net. the amber round and the pot's skim are the same rule at two sizes, set by the same hand, and the hand belongs to the consortium's partner from the counting house, who is also the raven at the pumps at dawn with a net, and the branded clerk from the kiln shed has the other half of the proof and you have the first. you traded ledgers sitting on a root in the last wood with the pickers sifting round you, and neither of you said the name aloud. you didn't need to. you've known it for three seasons without knowing it. the counting house brands a bird once. you've put a copy of both ledgers in the counting trees' hollow with the wardens' count, because the wardens don't hang anybody and don't give anybody up, and because a count kept there is a count nobody in the city can unwrite.",
		"thief": "square. the guild's cutters walked out of the wood with their knives and their sack and no names, and the wood has its count of the beads they took, and the wardens let them go at the causeway because the wardens don't keep birds. the oldest cutter told the guild what you said at the shelter: that a diver takes what the sea took, and the sea hadn't taken this. the guild has taken to quoting it as though it were always the guild's rule. you've stopped correcting them. you're square with the guild and the wood both, which nobody in the sump has managed before, and it's better than amber, and the kingfisher says it's going to be very useful at the spring turn, when the bell rings under the low town and the guild needs to look the other way while four birds go down to a vault.",
		"bard": "you have the count song, every tree and every name, the first winter's dead read off the wood they asked for and didn't get, and the choir's first winter verse is the same list from the other side. there's no number for the first tree. there's a pause where it would be, a breath held for one name, and you've sung it with the wardens at the counting trees with your hands in your pockets and the reader beside you calling the numbers you'd have missed. you're going to sing it at the pot at dawn, once, where the choir sings the monday names, so the city hears whose names they are. the wardens chose you because you've already got a writ on you. the reader says the city won't like it, and that this is the point. the choir master has sent the boy with a second slip: the monday names, and under them, in his own hand, the time.",
		"monk": "a heron's rule. the abbot walked out of the house once, and stayed a season in the wood, and kept a fire on dropped wood that the wardens never asked him to count, and when he went home he cut the first notch stick from the wood's ash and taught the house to read it, because he'd seen a fire burn with nobody counting it and it frightened him. the rule isn't holy and it isn't bought. it's a frightened bird's, and the counting house found it and has been reading it since. you carried the ember back to the sump in a clay pot, from the fire that's never been counted, and lit your fires from it, and they burn every night as they always did. you've written to the abbot, the first time you've written to him. you've told him where the ember came from, and that you understand now, and asked him to come and stand at one of your fires.",
		"knight": "the commander put it inside his coat. he didn't tear it and he didn't file it, and the order hasn't spoken of it since, and the gilt's fireplaces still burn oak, and the writ against the wood is still in the chapter house, unserved. but the order knows that a knight carried the answer up the stair and laid it on the table, and the answer is the tree every knight sees from the top and none of them has ever written down. you've broken the vigil's silence once, at a warden's fire, and the reader said every knight sees it. you think every knight has known what the order owes the wood and kept it under the silence with the nothing at the top of the spire. you've stopped keeping it. the wardens have given you a strip of bark with the first tree's bearings on it, for the next knight who carries the writ out, so they'll know where to stand.",
	},
}
